Algorithm: Next Smaller Element

Step 1: Create an empty stack to help find the next smaller elements.
Step 2: Create a list to store the result.
Step 3: Traverse the array from right to left.
Step 4: For each element, check if the stack is empty.
Step 5: If the stack is empty, add -1 to the result (no smaller element exists).
Step 6: If the stack is not empty, remove elements from the stack while they are greater than or equal to the current element.
Step 7: After removing, check the stack again.
Step 8: If the stack becomes empty, add -1 to the result; otherwise, add the top element of the stack as the next smaller element.
Step 9: Push the current element into the stack.
Step 10: Repeat this process for all elements.
Step 11: Reverse the result list to maintain the correct order.
Step 12: Return the final list.

Time Complexity: O(n)
Space Complexity: O(n)



-----> Code <-----

class Solution {
    static ArrayList<Integer> nextSmallerEle(int[] arr) {
        ArrayList<Integer> al=new ArrayList<>();
        Stack<Integer> st=new Stack<>();
        int n=arr.length;
        for(int i=n-1;i>=0;i--){
            if(st.isEmpty()){
                al.add(-1);
            }else{
                while(!st.isEmpty() && st.peek()>=arr[i]){
                    st.pop();
                }
                if(st.isEmpty())al.add(-1);
                else al.add(st.peek());
            }
            st.push(arr[i]);
        }
        Collections.reverse(al);
        return al;
    }
}
